## Authentication and Configuration

Chainguard API calls require authentication. By default, the provider obtains a
Chainguard token in one of two ways:

- **Ambient credentials** — the token that `chainctl auth login` caches on
  disk. Best for local, interactive use.
- **An assumable identity** — the provider exchanges an OIDC token from your
  environment for a Chainguard token. Best for CI and other automation, where
  no human is present to run a browser login.

You configure both through the optional `login_options` block.

### Interactive authentication

Log in once with `chainctl`:

```shell
chainctl auth login
```

The provider reuses that cached token, so you don't need to configure anything:

```terraform
provider "chainguard" {}
```

To have the provider open a browser and refresh the token itself when it
expires, configure the login flow. Set `auth0_connection` to the social
connection you sign in with:

```terraform
provider "chainguard" {
  login_options {
    # One of: google-oauth2, github, gitlab.
    auth0_connection      = "google-oauth2"
    enable_refresh_tokens = true
  }
}
```

The provider runs this flow itself, so you don't need to install `chainctl` to
authenticate.

If your organization uses a custom identity provider, select it with
`organization_name` (your verified organization) or `identity_provider_id`
instead of `auth0_connection`.

### Authenticating as an assumable identity

An assumable identity is a Chainguard identity that a workload authenticates as
by presenting an OIDC token, instead of one a human logs in to. The identity
defines which token claims it accepts; any environment that can mint a matching
OIDC token can then assume it.

This section assumes the identity already exists. Create one with the
[`chainguard_identity` resource](resources/identity.md) or with
`chainctl iam identities create`. To look up existing identities and their
UIDPs, use the [`chainguard_identity` data source](data-sources/identity.md) or
`chainctl iam identities list`.

To authenticate as the identity, set `identity_token` to the OIDC token to
exchange, as either a file path or the token string itself. Optionally set
`identity_id` to the UIDP of the identity to assume, which pins the exchange to
one identity:

```terraform
provider "chainguard" {
  login_options {
    identity_id    = "<identity-uidp>"
    identity_token = "/path/to/oidc/token"
  }
}
```

Most CI systems that support OIDC write a token to a file or expose one through
an environment variable. Point `identity_token` at that token. For example,
when the token is available as an environment variable, pass it through a
Terraform variable rather than hardcoding it:

```terraform
variable "oidc_token" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
}

provider "chainguard" {
  login_options {
    identity_id    = "<identity-uidp>"
    identity_token = var.oidc_token
  }
}
```

```shell
export TF_VAR_oidc_token="$(get-oidc-token)"
terraform apply
```

An identity needs a role binding before it can act on your resources. Grant one
with the [`chainguard_rolebinding` resource](resources/rolebinding.md).

#### HCP Terraform

HCP Terraform issues a workload identity token for each plan and apply, but
only as an environment variable, which a Terraform configuration can't read.
The provider reads it for you. Set the `TFC_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_AUDIENCE`
environment variable on the workspace to `https://issuer.enforce.dev`, and set
only `identity_id` in the provider block:

```terraform
provider "chainguard" {
  login_options {
    identity_id = "<identity-uidp>"
  }
}
```

The identity must trust the issuer `https://app.terraform.io`, and its subject
pattern must match both run phases, for example
`^organization:<org>:project:[^:]+:workspace:<workspace>:run_phase:(plan|apply)$`.

The provider uses the untagged `TFC_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_TOKEN` only when its
audience is `https://issuer.enforce.dev`. Otherwise it logs a warning and
ignores the token, so it never sends a token minted for another tool to
Chainguard. If another tool in the workspace already uses
`TFC_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_AUDIENCE`, set `TFC_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_AUDIENCE_CHAINGUARD`
instead. The provider prefers the resulting
`TFC_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_TOKEN_CHAINGUARD` over the untagged token. The provider
uses an HCP Terraform token only when `TF_CHAINGUARD_IDENTITY_TOKEN`, ambient
credentials, and `identity_token` are all unset.

Plan-phase tokens get the identity's full access, and VCS-driven workspaces run
speculative plans on pull requests by default. Code in a pull request can then
act with that access before anyone reviews it. Grant the identity only the
roles the configuration needs, and turn off automatic speculative plans on any
workspace whose identity can change IAM.

#### Constraints

- `identity_token` must be an OIDC token from an external issuer, such as your
  CI system. A Chainguard token, such as the output of `chainctl auth token`,
  is already the result of an exchange, and the exchange rejects it. Without
  `identity_id`, the error is `unsupported audience(s)`. With `identity_id`,
  the error names the first check the token fails, such as the issuer or a
  claim the identity requires.
- `identity_token` is exclusive. You cannot combine it with `auth0_connection`,
  `identity_provider_id`, `organization_name`, or `enable_refresh_tokens` —
  those configure the interactive browser flow, which the token exchange
  replaces. Refresh tokens are a browser-flow feature and do not apply.
- `identity_token` is a sensitive value. Source it from a CI secret or a
  short-lived OIDC token; never commit it.
- If you omit `identity_id`, token refreshes reuse the identity the current
  session was minted for. Set it to pin a specific identity, or to keep token
  caches separate when several identities share one environment.

### Verifying authentication

A provider block on its own authenticates nothing. Terraform configures a
provider only when a resource or data source needs it, so a configuration that
contains only a `provider` block plans as "No changes" without ever logging in.

To confirm your credentials work, add a read-only data source and an output:

```terraform
data "chainguard_group" "group" {
  name = "<organization>"
}

output "group_id" {
  value = data.chainguard_group.group.id
}
```

Then run:

```shell
terraform plan
```

The `plan` command reads data sources, which forces the provider to
authenticate — you don't need to apply. If your credentials work, the plan
resolves `group_id` to the group's UIDP. If they don't, the provider returns an
authentication error instead of a silent no-op.

Two commands help when a login fails:

- `TF_LOG=INFO terraform plan` prints the provider's login logs, including the
  `login_options` it parsed, so you can see which flow ran.
- `chainctl auth status` reports which identity your ambient credentials belong
  to.

### Disabling automatic login

`disabled` stops the provider from getting a new Chainguard token by any
method, including a token exchange. The provider then uses only a token that's
already cached, and fails fast with `automatic auth disabled` when none is
valid:

```terraform
provider "chainguard" {
  login_options {
    disabled = true
  }
}
```

Don't combine `disabled` with `identity_token`, because the exchange never
runs. You don't need `disabled` to keep CI from opening a browser: when the
provider has an identity token, a failed exchange returns an error and the
provider doesn't fall back to the browser flow.

### Proxy-managed authentication

Set `TF_CHAINGUARD_PROXY_AUTH=true` only when a trusted outbound proxy
authenticates each Chainguard API request. In this mode the provider sends no
Authorization header and does not load, refresh, or cache a Chainguard token.
Without that proxy, API calls fail authentication. The proxy must restrict
which requests receive credentials; this setting does not grant permissions.

